### A Record-breaking Year for Food Recalls
The year 2026 has been one for the books already, with 327 recalls to date. This compares starkly to the 685 recalls recorded over all time, illustrating an alarming trend of accelerating food safety incidents. April alone saw 84 recalls, a number that highlights the increasing frequency of such events.

### Repeat Offenders: When Will They Learn?
Among brands, Moonlight Packing Corporation and Albertsons Companies LLC lead the pack of worst offenders with 11 recalls each, mostly Class I (the most severe). Their respective grades, F, indicate a systemic issue in compliance or oversight. Notably, CHETAK NEW YORK LLC tops the recall count with 52 recalls, all Class I incidents involving serious health risks.

### Allergens and Mislabeling: A Persistent Issue
Undeclared allergens, such as peanuts, sesame, and milk proteins, continue to dominate recall reasons. A shining example--or rather an alarming one--is Costco's Kirkland Signature Madeleines being pulled for undeclared hazelnut and soy lecithin. Mislabeling, while less common, poses a critical risk, as evident in Booey's Dragon's Breath sauce failing to disclose fish and sesame allergens.

### Contaminants: A Growing Threat
Salmonella contamination led Unistel Industries to recall their Ground All Spice, a Class I recall. Meanwhile, Summit Import Corporation faced a Class II recall due to excessive lead levels in its Spice Powder. These recurring issues underscore the need for stringent testing protocols.

### Consumer Tips: Staying Safe in the Recall Era
To navigate the recall wave, always check the FDA's website for updates, especially if consuming products from repeat offenders or high-risk categories like packaged foods and spices. Stay informed; your health deserves nothing less.
